  "text": "The study examined a medium‑carbon steel with low manganese‑chromium content that was alloyed with varying amounts of phosphorus, molybdenum and titanium. First, the role of dissolved nitrogen and the effect of tying up nitrogen by adding titanium on the steel’s tendency to become brittle after tempering were explored. Next, the influence of typical commercial levels of phosphorus and molybdenum on this brittleness was assessed. A second heat‑treatment step (re‑tempering) was then applied to see how it altered the brittleness, and the relationship between the brittleness and the temperature at which the material’s impact resistance drops (impact transition temperature) was investigated. Finally, any link between the brittleness and the material’s electrical resistance was checked. The findings showed that low‑temperature brittleness (around 250‑350 °C) is an irreversible change linked to martensite breakdown and differs from high‑temperature brittleness (around 450‑550 °C), which appears after re‑tempering due to new precipitates that raise the impact transition temperature. Raising phosphorus increased this transition temperature, but adding molybdenum effectively countered the rise; a molybdenum‑to‑phosphorus ratio above about 10 was needed to avoid high‑temperature brittleness. No connection was found between brittleness and electrical resistance."
}
